sigma sieve to 10000000: 11.4s

==============================================================================
A. Proposition 1 (parity alternation of s'(n) = sigma(n) - n - 1) and corollaries
==============================================================================
   Blind re-derivation.  sigma is multiplicative; sigma(2^a) = 2^(a+1) - 1 is odd; for an odd prime q,
   sigma(q^e) = 1 + q + ... + q^e has e + 1 terms, all odd, so sigma(q^e) = e + 1 (mod 2).  Hence
   sigma(n) odd  <=>  all odd-prime exponents even  <=>  the odd part of n is a square  <=>  n = k^2 or 2k^2.
   n even: s'(n) = sigma(n) - n - 1 = sigma(n) + 1 (mod 2): s'(n) odd <=> sigma(n) even <=> odd part not a square.
   n odd : s'(n) = sigma(n) - 1 - 1 = sigma(n)     (mod 2): s'(n) even <=> sigma(n) even <=> n not a square.
   In both cases  s'(n) = n (mod 2)  <=>  the odd part of n is a square  ('square type').  Prop 1 (a), (b) hold.
   (c) a fixed point or an odd cycle cannot flip parity at every step (an odd number of flips returns to the
   opposite parity), so it contains a square-type element; a same-parity 2-cycle has s'(n) = n (mod 2) at both
   members; #{n <= N square type} = sum_a #{k odd: 2^a k^2 <= N} ~ N^(1/2)/(2 - sqrt 2) = O(N^(1/2)).  All correct.
   [OK  ] classical fact: sigma(n) odd iff n is a square or twice a square, all 1 <= n <= 2000000
   [OK  ] Prop 1 (a)+(b) as one statement: s'(n) = n (mod 2) iff odd part of n is a square, all 2 <= n <= 2000000
   [OK  ] Prop 1 (a): n even, s'(n) odd iff the odd part is not a square
   [OK  ] Prop 1 (b): n odd, s'(n) even iff n is not a square
   [OK  ] no fixed point of s' (= quasiperfect number) with n <= 2000000
   Parity persistence in [M, 2M).  By the theorem the persisting n are EXACTLY the square-type n, so the
   'measured' proportion is a deterministic count; its asymptotic is sqrt(1/(2M)) among all n (k odd in 2^a k^2).
   M         #sqtype  share(all n)  share(composites)  sqrt(1/(2M))  sqrt(1/(2M))*M/#comp  note .out
   1000           22  0.02200       0.02543            0.02236       0.02585               0.02543
   [OK  ] M = 1000: square-type count by enumeration = 22; persisting composites = square-type composites = 22
   10000          71  0.00710       0.00792            0.00707       0.00789               0.00792
   [OK  ] M = 10000: square-type count by enumeration = 71; persisting composites = square-type composites = 71
   100000        224  0.00224       0.00245            0.00224       0.00244               0.00245
   [OK  ] M = 100000: square-type count by enumeration = 224; persisting composites = square-type composites = 224
   1000000       707  0.00071       0.00076            0.00071       0.00076               0.00076
   [OK  ] M = 1000000: square-type count by enumeration = 707; persisting composites = square-type composites = 707
   Cattaneo's theorem (quasiperfect => odd square) is STRONGER than Prop 1(c): it also excludes n twice a square.
   Argument: n = 2^a m^2 (a odd, m odd), sigma(n) = (2^(a+1) - 1) sigma(m^2) = 2^(a+1) m^2 + 1 gives
   m^2 = -1 (mod 2^(a+1) - 1), but 2^(a+1) - 1 = 3 (mod 4) has a prime factor = 3 (mod 4): impossible.
   [OK  ] checked a = 1, 3, ..., 39: 2^(a+1) - 1 = 3 (mod 4) and has a prime factor = 3 (mod 4)

